{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "i55",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Typing a property on an object or tag should show help for it, as you type",
      "excerpt": "Typing a property on an object or tag should show help for it, as you type",
      "text": "Is it possible to show a custom menu here?  e.g. for `color`, we could show the color pallete, `icon` could show the icon picker",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T09:41:54.213Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T09:42:38.563Z",
          "html": ""
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T09:42:50.281Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, though not inside Monaco's suggestion list itself, which only draws rows of text. What works is the editor opening its own popup at the cursor, with the same pickers the Inspector already uses for these properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>color=\"|\"</code>:  the color palette, the theme's named colours first</li>\n<li><code>icon=\"|\"</code>:  the icon picker, with search</li>\n<li>any other attribute whose type has a picker in the property editor (a one-of as pills ...) can open the same way</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choosing writes the value between the quotes as one undo step, and Esc goes back to typing.  The word list stays as it is for people who type.</p>\n<p>Filed as <a href=\"#i112\">I112</a>.</p>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "at": "2026-11-14T09:55:27.111Z",
          "text": "NOTE: this should be a `todo` rather than an `issue`"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T10:03:51.548Z",
          "html": "<p>Done:  it is <a href=\"#t61\">T61</a> now, a todo, and I112 is struck through pointing at it.  From here on a new feature you ask for is filed as a todo, an issue only for something broken.  An agent is building T61.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-14T09:42:37.796Z"
      }
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "i61",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "P29: an <action> inside a drawing's <if ...> lost its action, drawing a plain button",
      "excerpt": "P29: an <action> inside a drawing's <if ...> lost its action, drawing a plain button",
      "text": "Maybe \n```\nto xxxx\n   disabled when yyyy\n```",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T09:43:40.865Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "at": "2026-11-14T09:57:13.209Z",
          "text": "do this"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T09:58:10.768Z",
          "html": "<p>Yes, and it's Spell's syntax, so it went to <code>language</code> as <epic-link epic=\"language\" item=\"T132\"></epic-link>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>disabled when …</code> under an action's head, beside <code>enabled when …</code>, and both read:  <code>disabled when x</code> compiles to <code>enabled when not (x)</code></li>\n<li>Spanish:  <code>deshabilitado cuando …</code></li>\n<li>the translator writes both ways, and the examples switch where a negated <code>enabled when</code> reads better</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Nothing changes in the editor:  it reads the compiled action's enabled state, so its rows and buttons grey the same.  It shows on main when language merges it.</p>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T10:03:52.843Z",
          "html": "<p>On it:  <code>language</code> is building it as <epic-link epic=\"language\" item=\"T132\"></epic-link>, and merges it into main when it is green;  I will say here when it is in.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-14T09:58:10.075Z"
      }
    },
    "c4": {
      "anchor": "i70",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Choosing a tag from the list writes it whole: <act + Tab gives <action \"\"/>, the caret between the quotes",
      "excerpt": "Choosing a tag from the list writes it whole: <act + Tab gives <action \"\"/>, the caret between the quotes",
      "text": "good.  Let's add a space between the end quote and the |>",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T09:51:48.051Z",
      "status": "new"
    },
    "c5": {
      "anchor": "i72",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "The Spell menu: Home and New Example at the top; Home out of the project's menu; no caret after the project's name",
      "excerpt": "The Spell menu: Home and New Example at the top; Home out of the project's menu; no caret after the project's name",
      "text": "Put `<pen> New Example` at the end of the list of examples.  Move \"About Spell\" in the same row with Home",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-14T09:52:57.872Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-14T10:03:50.092Z",
          "html": "<p>Done, as <a href=\"#i114\">I114</a>.  Home and About Spell… share the menu's top row, side by side when the examples take columns, stacked when they don't.  Both are drawn by <code>SpellEditor.spell</code>'s new <code>home row</code>, so you can tweak them there.  New Example, with its pen, is now the last row of the examples, inside their columns.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-14T10:03:48.545Z"
      }
    }
  },
